 { White's last move was 23.e4-e5. Evaluate this move and describe how the game may develop. Penteala Harikrishna – Leinier Dominguez, Melilla 2011 As explained in Chapter 7, the move e4-e5 can be useful to create mating threats. But in an endgame, this move can be more weakening and, as in the game Vovk – Volokitin, we would expect Black to establish a blockade on d5 and then create a decisive passed pawn on the queenside. This is exactly what happened after: } 23. e5 Be7 24. Nc3 Nc6 25. Be4 ( { An option was } 25. Be3 Nb4 26. Bb1 Nd5 27. Nxd5 Bxd5 $15 { when Black will transfer his king to the queenside and then advance his majority. } ) 
Bb4 $1 { To gain control of d5. } 26. Bxc6 Bxc6 27. Nd1 Be7 28. f3 f5 $5 { Preventing the manoeuvre Nf2-e4, as well as a possible kingside expansion. } 29. Nf2 ( { White would also be in trouble after: } 29. exf6 Bxf6 30. Be3 b5 31. a3 a5 32. Kf2 Kf7 $17 ) 
Bd5 $17 { Black has blocked the d-pawn and now is ready to create a passed pawn on the queenside. } 30. a3 Bc4 { Preventing 31.Nd3. } 31. Nh3 b5 32. Kf2 a5 33. Ke1 a4 34. Kd2 b4 35. axb4 Bxb4+ 36. Kc2 a3 37. Nf4 g5 38. Nd3 a2 39. Bb2 Bf8 40. Nc5 Bxh6 $19 { Black has a clear extra pawn and the game concluded with: } 41. Kc3 Bd5 42. Nd7 Bg7 43. Nb6 h5 44. Nxd5 exd5 45. Kb3 g4 46. Kxa2 Bh6 { To follow up with 47...gxf3 and promoting the h-pawn. } 
