 { Evaluate the position and suggest a plan for White. Jorge Cori Tello – Hrant Melkumyan, Moscow 2012 We have a Carlsbad structure where White has played b2-b3 instead of the more usual c2-c3. In Chapter 5 we studied an identical structure in the game Wojtaszek – Khairullin, but with reversed colours. According to that game, Black’s plans should be based on playing ...a7-a5, ...b6-b5-b4 and then either ...Ba6 to trade bishops and pressure c2, or break in the centre with ...e6-e5. As the reader may notice, neither plan works here. Despite the level appearance of the position, Black experiences some difficulties already because he lacks a plan. The game continued: } 12. { -KEY- } Qe2 Rc8 13. f4 Qc7 14. Rf2 Ng6 15. g3 Qe7 16. h4 $1 { Beginning a kingside attack; note how Black simply marks time. } Rfe8 ( { Or } 16... Qd8 17. h5 Ne7 18. g4 $1 { preventing ...Nf5; there is no counterplay and White may prepare a kingside assault for as long as he needs to. } ) 
17. h5 Nf8 18. g4 N6d7 19. g5 $1 { An excellent decision that entails a strong pawn sacrifice; Black’s forces are paralyzed. } Bxe5 $6 { This accelerates defeat. } ( { Sturdier was waiting with } 19... Rc7 { though after } 20. Qe3 Rec8 21. Ndf3 Qe8 22. Rg2 $16 { the eventual g5-g6 break will be strong. } ) 
20. fxe5 { This is the point – the g5-pawn can be sacrificed. } ( { But not } 20. dxe5 $2 Nc5 { when Black gains counterplay. } ) 
Qxg5+ 21. Rg2 Qe7 ( { Or } 21... Qh4 22. Nf3 Qxh5 23. Rh2 Qg4 24. Kf2 { followed by 25.Rg1 } Qf4 26. Bc1 { trapping the queen. } ) 
22. Nf3 $18 { White has a crushing attack, and the game finished convincingly: } f6 23. Bc1 fxe5 24. Bg5 Qd6 25. Nxe5 Nxe5 26. dxe5 Qc5+ 27. Kh2 d4 28. Rg3 Rc7 29. Rag1 Rf7 30. Bf6 Ree7 31. Qg4 Qc7 32. Bxe7 Qxe7 33. Qxd4 Nd7 34. h6 Nxe5 35. Kh3 Ng6 36. Bxg6 hxg6 37. Rxg6 Rf3+ 38. R1g3 Rxg3+ 39. Rxg3 g5 40. Qe5 Kh7 41. Rxg5 Kxh6 42. Qh8+ $1 ( { Black resigned as the queen is lost after } 42. Qh8+ Kxg5 43. Qh4 Kf5 44. Qxe7 { . } ) 

 1-0

